        ],
        "statement": "Let $G$ be a topological group and let $(\\pi,H)$ be a strongly continuous unitary representation ([[def-strongly-continuous-unitary-representation]]). For a subset $Q\\subseteq G$ and a real number $\\varepsilon>0$, a vector $\\xi\\in H$ is **$(Q,\\varepsilon)$-invariant** if $$\\sup_{x\\in Q}\\lVert\\pi(x)\\xi-\\xi\\rVert<\\varepsilon\\lVert\\xi\\rVert,$$ and the representation $\\pi$ **has almost invariant vectors** if for every compact $Q\\subseteq G$ ([[def-compact-space]]) and every $\\varepsilon>0$ there is a $(Q,\\varepsilon)$-invariant unit vector. The scaling by $\\lVert\\xi\\rVert$ is immaterial for unit vectors, so the condition on unit vectors is the familiar one $\\sup_{x\\in Q}\\lVert\\pi(x)\\xi-\\xi\\rVert<\\varepsilon$. When $G$ is locally compact Hausdorff ([[def-locally-compact-space]]), [[lem-weak-containment-of-the-trivial-representation-and-almost-invariant-vectors]] identifies this condition with $1_G\\prec\\pi$ ([[def-weak-containment-of-unitary-representations]]); for an arbitrary topological group only the implication 'almost invariant vectors $\\Rightarrow$ $1_G\\prec\\pi$' holds, the converse being where local compactness enters. No choice principle is used in the definition.",
        "strategy": "Definition: the $(Q,\\varepsilon)$-condition is invariant under nonzero scaling, so '$(Q,\\varepsilon)$-invariant vector' and '$(Q,\\varepsilon)$-invariant unit vector' determine each other by normalisation. The identity with $1_G\\prec\\pi$ is quoted from the published lemma under its locally compact Hausdorff hypothesis; only the direction from almost invariant vectors is asserted for a general topological group, and the source's remark records that the converse fails without local compactness. No further assertion is made and no choice principle is used.",
