      {
        "id": "def-kazhdan-pair-and-kazhdan-constant",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Kazhdan pairs, Kazhdan sets and Kazhdan constants",
        "deps": [
          "def-almost-invariant-vectors-for-a-unitary-representation",
          "def-strongly-continuous-unitary-representation",
          "def-hilbert-space",
          "def-topological-group",
          "def-infimum"
        ],
        "statement": "Let $G$ be a topological group. A pair $(Q,\\varepsilon)$ with $Q\\subseteq G$ and $\\varepsilon>0$ is a **Kazhdan pair** for $G$ if every strongly continuous unitary representation of $G$ having a $(Q,\\varepsilon)$-invariant unit vector ([[def-almost-invariant-vectors-for-a-unitary-representation]]) has a nonzero $G$-invariant vector. A subset $Q\\subseteq G$ is a **Kazhdan set** if $(Q,\\varepsilon)$ is a Kazhdan pair for some $\\varepsilon>0$. For $Q\\subseteq G$ and a unitary representation $\\pi$ the **displacement constant** is $$\\kappa(G,Q,\\pi):=\\inf\\bigl\\{\\sup_{x\\in Q}\\lVert\\pi(x)\\xi-\\xi\\rVert:\\xi\\in H,\\ \\lVert\\xi\\rVert=1\\bigr\\},$$ an infimum in $[0,2]$, with the convention $\\inf\\varnothing=+\\infty$ when $H=0$; and the **Kazhdan constant** of $Q$ is $$\\kappa(G,Q):=\\sup\\{\\varepsilon>0:(Q,\\varepsilon)\\text{ is a Kazhdan pair for }G\\}.$$ With these definitions $(Q,\\varepsilon)$ is a Kazhdan pair if and only if $\\kappa(G,Q)\\ge\\varepsilon$, and for every unitary representation $\\pi$ of $G$ without nonzero invariant vector one has $\\kappa(G,Q,\\pi)\\ge\\kappa(G,Q)$, with equality of the optimal constant $\\kappa(G,Q)=\\inf_{\\pi}\\kappa(G,Q,\\pi)$ understood in the sense of that universal lower bound.",
        "strategy": "Definition plus two elementary identifications. (1) If $(Q,\\varepsilon)$ is a Kazhdan pair and $0<\\varepsilon'\\le\\varepsilon$, every $(Q,\\varepsilon')$-invariant vector is $(Q,\\varepsilon)$-invariant, so $(Q,\\varepsilon')$ is again a Kazhdan pair; hence the set of admissible $\\varepsilon$ is an interval and $\\kappa(G,Q)$ is its supremum. (2) If $(Q,\\varepsilon)$ is a Kazhdan pair and $\\pi$ has no nonzero invariant vector, then $\\pi$ has no $(Q,\\varepsilon)$-invariant unit vector, that is $\\kappa(G,Q,\\pi)\\ge\\varepsilon$; taking the supremum over $\\varepsilon$ gives $\\kappa(G,Q,\\pi)\\ge\\kappa(G,Q)$, and conversely if $\\kappa(G,Q,\\pi)\\ge\\varepsilon$ for every such $\\pi$, then no such $\\pi$ has a $(Q,\\varepsilon)$-invariant unit vector, so $(Q,\\varepsilon)$ is a Kazhdan pair. The arguments use only the definitions and the order structure of the reals ([[def-infimum]]); no choice principle is used.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
