        "statement": "Let $G$ be a topological group. Then $G$ has **Kazhdan's property (T)** if every strongly continuous unitary representation of $G$ which has almost invariant vectors ([[def-almost-invariant-vectors-for-a-unitary-representation]]) has a nonzero $G$-invariant vector. Equivalently: whenever a strongly continuous unitary representation $(\\pi,H)$ of $G$ has, for every compact $Q\\subseteq G$ and every $\\varepsilon>0$, a unit vector $\\xi$ with $\\sup_{x\\in Q}\\lVert\\pi(x)\\xi-\\xi\\rVert<\\varepsilon$, then $\\pi$ has a nonzero vector fixed by every $\\pi(g)$. This is the definition used throughout this page; the equivalent formulation by compact Kazhdan sets is [[thm-property-t-is-equivalent-to-the-existence-of-a-kazhdan-pair]].",
        "strategy": "Definition: the two displayed formulations are the same condition, by the definition of almost invariant vectors and of a nonzero invariant vector. No choice principle is used.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
