        "dependency_level": 1
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-property-t-is-equivalent-to-the-existence-of-a-kazhdan-pair",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "title": "Property (T) is equivalent to the existence of a compact Kazhdan pair",
        "deps": [
          "def-almost-invariant-vectors-for-a-unitary-representation",
          "def-kazhdan-pair-and-kazhdan-constant",
          "def-kazhdans-property-t",
          "def-hilbert-direct-sum-of-unitary-representations",
          "thm-orthogonal-decomposition-by-a-closed-subspace",
          "def-orthogonality-and-orthogonal-complement",
          "lem-orthogonal-projection-is-linear-self-adjoint-contractive",
          "def-strongly-continuous-unitary-representation",
          "def-hilbert-space",
          "def-compact-space",
          "def-topological-group",
          "def-axiom-of-choice"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $G$ be a topological group. The following are equivalent: (i) $G$ has property (T) ([[def-kazhdans-property-t]]); (ii) $G$ has a compact Kazhdan set, that is, there are a compact $Q\\subseteq G$ and an $\\varepsilon>0$ such that $(Q,\\varepsilon)$ is a Kazhdan pair ([[def-kazhdan-pair-and-kazhdan-constant]]); (iii) there is a compact $Q\\subseteq G$ with $\\kappa(G,Q)>0$. Moreover, if $(Q,\\varepsilon)$ is a Kazhdan pair, $\\pi$ is any unitary representation of $G$ and $\\xi\\in H$ is $(Q,\\delta\\varepsilon)$-invariant for some $0<\\delta\\le1$, then $\\lVert\\xi-P\\xi\\rVert\\le\\delta\\lVert\\xi\\rVert$, where $P$ is the orthogonal projection of $H$ onto the closed subspace $H^G$ of $G$-invariant vectors.",
        "strategy": "The equivalence of (ii) and (iii) is the definition of $\\kappa(G,Q)$ as the supremum of the admissible $\\varepsilon$. (ii) implies (i): if $\\pi$ has almost invariant vectors, choose a $(Q,\\varepsilon)$-invariant unit vector; the Kazhdan pair property gives a nonzero invariant vector. (i) implies (ii): suppose no compact Kazhdan set exists, so that for every compact $Q$ and every $\\varepsilon>0$ there is a unitary representation $\\pi_{Q,\\varepsilon}$ without nonzero invariant vector and with a $(Q,\\varepsilon)$-invariant unit vector. Form the Hilbert direct sum $\\pi=\\widehat{\\bigoplus}_{(Q,\\varepsilon)}\\pi_{Q,\\varepsilon}$ over the set of pairs; given compact $Q$ and $\\varepsilon>0$ the embedded witness of the pair $(Q,\\varepsilon/2)$ has displacement $<\\varepsilon$ on $Q$, so $\\pi$ has almost invariant vectors. By (i) $\\pi$ has a nonzero invariant vector; its components are invariant because the coordinate projections intertwine ([[def-hilbert-direct-sum-of-unitary-representations]]), contradicting the choice of the $\\pi_{Q,\\varepsilon}$. The quantitative clause is the proof of Bekka-de la Harpe-Valette, Proposition 1.1.9: write $\\xi=\\xi'+\\xi''$ with $\\xi'=P\\xi$ and $\\xi''\\in(H^G)^\\perp$ ([[thm-orthogonal-decomposition-by-a-closed-subspace]]); $H^G$ is closed and $G$-invariant, so $(H^G)^\\perp$ carries a unitary representation with no nonzero invariant vector, and by the definition of $\\kappa(G,Q)$ one has $\\sup_{x\\in Q}\\lVert\\pi(x)\\xi''-\\xi''\\rVert\\ge\\kappa(G,Q)\\,\\lVert\\xi''\\rVert\\ge\\varepsilon\\lVert\\xi''\\rVert$. On the other hand $\\pi(x)\\xi''-\\xi''=\\pi(x)\\xi-\\xi$ because $\\xi'$ is $G$-invariant, so the left side is $<\\delta\\varepsilon\\lVert\\xi\\rVert$; comparing gives the claim. Orthogonal projection facts are [[lem-orthogonal-projection-is-linear-self-adjoint-contractive]] and [[def-orthogonality-and-orthogonal-complement]]. The Axiom of Choice is used to select the representations and witnesses indexed by the pairs $(Q,\\varepsilon)$ ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]).",
