      {
        "id": "thm-property-t-is-equivalent-to-isolation-of-the-trivial-representation",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "title": "Property (T) and isolation of the trivial representation in the Fell dual",
        "deps": [
          "def-kazhdans-property-t",
          "def-almost-invariant-vectors-for-a-unitary-representation",
          "def-fell-topology-on-the-unitary-dual",
          "def-unitary-dual-of-a-locally-compact-group",
          "lem-fell-closure-is-characterized-by-weak-containment",
          "def-weak-containment-of-unitary-representations",
          "def-hilbert-direct-sum-of-unitary-representations",
          "lem-weak-containment-of-the-trivial-representation-and-almost-invariant-vectors",
          "def-axiom-of-choice"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $G$ be a locally compact Hausdorff group. Then $G$ has property (T) ([[def-kazhdans-property-t]]) if and only if the class $[1_G]$ of the trivial representation is isolated in the Fell topology on the unitary dual $\\widehat G$ ([[def-fell-topology-on-the-unitary-dual]], [[def-unitary-dual-of-a-locally-compact-group]]). Equivalently: $G$ has property (T) if and only if for every set $R$ of unitary equivalence classes of strongly continuous unitary representations of $G$ with $\\widehat G\\subseteq R$, whose classes outside $[1_G]$ all have no nonzero invariant vector, the class $[1_G]$ is isolated in $R$.",
        "strategy": "The implication (T) $\\Rightarrow$ isolation is complete and runs as follows: if $[1_G]$ is not isolated in $\\widehat G$, then $1_G$ lies in the Fell closure of $\\widehat G\\setminus\\{[1_G]\\}$; by [[lem-fell-closure-is-characterized-by-weak-containment]] this gives $1_G\\prec\\widehat\\bigoplus_{\\sigma\\not\\cong1_G}\\sigma$, and property (T) together with [[lem-weak-containment-of-the-trivial-representation-and-almost-invariant-vectors]] yields $1_G\\subset\\widehat\\bigoplus_{\\sigma}\\sigma$; a nonzero invariant vector in a Hilbert direct sum has a nonzero invariant coordinate, and an irreducible representation with a nonzero invariant vector is the trivial one, a contradiction. The same argument proves the $R$-form, choosing a class $\\rho\\in R\\setminus\\{[1_G]\\}$ in every basic Fell neighbourhood of $[1_G]$ and forming the direct sum over $R$. The converse (isolation $\\Rightarrow$ (T)) is not proved here: in the source it is Bekka-de la Harpe-Valette Lemma 1.2.4 and Theorem 1.2.5, whose proof uses the weak-* density of the convex hull of the pure normalized positive type functions in $P_1(G)$ (Bekka-de la Harpe-Valette Theorem C.5.5(ii)), which in turn rests on the identification of the weak-* compact convex set $P^{\\le1}(G)$ with classes of positive type functions (Lemma C.5.3, referred by the source to Folland (3.35) and Dixmier 13.4.4), followed by Raikov's theorem, a finite-dimensional splitting argument, and Milman's theorem. The library supplies Raikov's theorem, Milman's converse, Banach-Alaoglu, and the extreme-point description of irreducible vector functionals, but not the two density/identification results; without them no complete local proof of the converse is available, so this item is escalated. No weakening of the statement is made.",
