        "statement": "Let $P^1(\\mathbb R)$ be the set of one-dimensional linear subspaces of $\\mathbb R^2$, written $[x:y]$, identified with the one-point compactification $\\mathbb R^*=\\mathbb R\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$ of $\\mathbb R$ ([[def-one-point-compactification]]) through $[t:1]\\leftrightarrow t$ and $[1:0]\\leftrightarrow\\infty$. By [[thm-one-point-compactification-properties]], $P^1(\\mathbb R)\\cong\\mathbb R^*$ is compact and Hausdorff, and inverse stereographic projection $h(t)=\\bigl(\\tfrac{2t}{t^2+1},\\tfrac{t^2-1}{t^2+1}\\bigr)$, $h(\\infty)=(0,1)$, is a homeomorphism of $\\mathbb R^*$ onto the unit circle $S^1$ ([[lem-unit-circle-is-a-compact-metrizable-topological-group]]); transporting the circle metric along $h$ makes $P^1(\\mathbb R)$ a compact metrizable space ([[def-metrizable-space]]). The map $\\varphi:\\mathbb R^2\\setminus\\{0\\}\\to P^1(\\mathbb R)$, $\\varphi(x,y)=x/y$ for $y\\ne0$ and $\\varphi(x,0)=\\infty$, is continuous and surjective, and $\\varphi(u,v)=\\varphi(x,y)$ if and only if $(u,v)=\\lambda(x,y)$ for some $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R^\\times$ ([[def-continuous-map-top]]). For $g=\\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{pmatrix}\\in\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb R)$ ([[ex-general-and-special-linear-lie-groups]]) put $g\\cdot[x:y]:=[ax+by:cx+dy]$. This is a well-defined left action of $\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb R)$ on $P^1(\\mathbb R)$ by homeomorphisms, continuous in both variables, and in the coordinate $t$ it is the Möbius formula $$g\\cdot t=\\frac{at+b}{ct+d}\\quad(ct+d\\ne0),\\qquad g\\cdot(-d/c)=\\infty,\\qquad g\\cdot\\infty=\\begin{cases}a/c,&c\\ne0,\\\\ \\infty,&c=0.\\end{cases}$$ In particular the unipotents $u_+=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}$ and $u_-=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix}$ satisfy $u_+\\cdot t=t+1$ for all $t\\in\\mathbb R$ with $u_+\\cdot\\infty=\\infty$, and $u_-\\cdot t=t/(t+1)$, so in the chart $s=y/x$ one has $u_-\\cdot s=s+1$ with $u_-\\cdot0=0$.",
        "strategy": "Well-definedness: if $(u,v)=\\lambda(x,y)$ with $\\lambda\\ne0$ then $[au+bv:cu+dv]=[\\lambda(ax+by):\\lambda(cx+dy)]$, so the action descends to lines; associativity is matrix multiplication. Topology: $h$ is a bijection onto $S^1$, with inverse $(x,y)\\mapsto x/(1-y)$ on $S^1\\setminus\\{(0,1)\\}$ and $(0,1)\\mapsto\\infty$, and it is continuous: on $\\mathbb R$ both coordinates are quotients of continuous functions with nonvanishing denominator $t^2+1$, and at $\\infty$ one has $\\lVert h(t)-(0,1)\\rVert=2/\\sqrt{t^2+1}\\to0$, the preimage of a ball about $(0,1)$ being a tail $\\{|t|>R\\}\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$, which is open in $\\mathbb R^*$ because $[-R,R]$ is compact ([[thm-heine-borel-r]], [[def-one-point-compactification]]); the same Thus $h$ is a continuous bijection from the compact Hausdorff space $\\mathbb R^*$ ([[thm-one-point-compactification-properties]]) onto the Hausdorff space $S^1$; closed subsets of $\\mathbb R^*$ are compact ([[thm-closed-subspace-of-a-compact-space-is-compact]]), their images under $h$ are compact ([[thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps]]) and hence closed in $S^1$ ([[thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed]]), so $h$ is a homeomorphism and the metric of $S^1$ transfers to $P^1(\\mathbb R)$ ([[def-metrizable-space]]). Continuity of $\\varphi$: on the open set $\\{y\\ne0\\}$ it is the ratio of continuous functions, and a basic neighbourhood of $\\infty$ has preimage $\\{y=0\\}\\cup\\{(x,y):y\\ne0,\\ x/y\\notin K\\}$ for compact $K\\subseteq\\mathbb R$, whose complement is closed in $\\mathbb R^2\\setminus\\{0\\}$ because a sequence with $y_n\\ne0$, $x_n/y_n\\in K$ converging to $(x,y)$ with $y=0$ would have $x\\ne0$ and $|x_n/y_n|\\to\\infty$, contradicting boundedness. Each $g$ acts as a homeomorphism because $g^{-1}$ implements the inverse map. Continuity of the action: on the open set where $ct+d\\ne0$ the coordinate formula is a quotient of continuous functions; near a pole $t=-d/c$ both sides tend to $\\infty$ in the one-point compactification, and the value at $\\infty$ is the limit $a/c$ of $t\\mapsto(at+b)/(ct+d)$ as $|t|\\to\\infty$ when $c\\ne0$, while for $c=0$ the formula is the continuous affine map $t\\mapsto(a t+b)/d$ extended by $\\infty\\mapsto\\infty$. The two unipotent computations are the displayed formulas with $(a,b,c,d)=(1,1,0,1)$ and $(1,0,1,1)$; the chart $s=y/x$ is exchanged with $t$ by the swap $[x:y]\\mapsto[y:x]$, which is the homeomorphism $t\\mapsto1/t$ of $P^1(\\mathbb R)$. No choice principle is used.",
