        "statement": "Let $P^1(\\mathbb R)$ be the real projective line with the natural action of $\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb R)$ ([[def-real-projective-line-and-its-sl2-action]]). Let $u_1,u_2\\in\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb R)$ be unipotent matrices, that is $u_i\\ne I$ and $(u_i-I)^2=0$, whose fixed lines in $\\mathbb R^2$ are distinct. Then there is no Borel probability measure on $P^1(\\mathbb R)$ ([[def-borel-sigma-algebra]], [[def-measure]], [[def-probability-measure]]) invariant under both $u_1$ and $u_2$. In particular no Borel probability measure is invariant under both $u_+=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}$ and $u_-=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix}$.",
        "strategy": "Choose a basis of $\\mathbb R^2$ whose first vector spans the fixed line of $u_1$ and whose second vector spans the fixed line of $u_2$. In this basis a unipotent with a given one-dimensional fixed line is $I+cE_{12}$ with $c\\ne0$ (respectively $I+c'E_{21}$ with $c'\\ne0$), because $(u-I)^2=0$, $u\\ne I$ and the kernel of $u-I$ is the fixed line; hence in the projective coordinate $t=x/y$ the first unipotent acts by $t\\mapsto t+c$ and fixes $\\infty$, while the second fixes $0$ and, in the chart $s=y/x$, acts by $s\\mapsto s+c'$. Let $\\nu$ be a probability invariant under the first unipotent, and let $\\nu_0$ be its restriction to the Borel set $\\mathbb R\\subseteq P^1(\\mathbb R)$; $\\nu_0$ is a finite measure and invariance gives $\\nu_0(A-c)=\\nu(A)$ for every Borel $A\\subseteq\\mathbb R$. The sets $A_k:=[kc,(k+1)c)$, $k\\in\\mathbb Z$, partition $\\mathbb R$ and all have equal $\\nu_0$-measure, so finiteness of $\\nu_0(\\mathbb R)$ forces $\\nu_0(\\mathbb R)=0$; hence $\\nu=\\delta_\\infty$. If $\\nu$ were also invariant under the second unipotent, then $\\delta_\\infty$ would be fixed by it, but the second unipotent moves $\\infty$ to $[1:c']\\ne[1:0]$. Contradiction. The argument is elementary measure theory on the line; no choice principle is used beyond the cited definitions.",
