        "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $G$ be a topological group, $N\\trianglelefteq G$ a closed normal subgroup and let $(Q,\\varepsilon)$ be a relative Kazhdan pair for $(G,N)$ ([[def-relative-property-t-for-a-pair]]): every strongly continuous unitary representation of $G$ with a $(Q,\\varepsilon)$-invariant unit vector has a nonzero $N$-invariant vector. Then for every strongly continuous unitary representation $(\\pi,H)$ of $G$ and every $\\xi\\in H$, $$\\operatorname{dist}(\\xi,H^N)\\le\\varepsilon^{-1}\\sup_{q\\in Q}\\lVert\\pi(q)\\xi-\\xi\\rVert,\\qquad \\sup_{n\\in N}\\lVert\\pi(n)\\xi-\\xi\\rVert\\le2\\varepsilon^{-1}\\sup_{q\\in Q}\\lVert\\pi(q)\\xi-\\xi\\rVert.$$ Moreover, if $(G,N)$ has relative property (T) and $Q\\subseteq G$ is compact with $\\bigcup_{k\\ge1}Q^k=G$, then there is $\\delta>0$ such that every unitary representation of $G$ without nonzero $N$-invariant vector satisfies $\\sup_{q\\in Q}\\lVert\\pi(q)\\xi-\\xi\\rVert\\ge\\delta\\lVert\\xi\\rVert$ for every $\\xi\\in H$.",
        "strategy": "Since $N$ is normal, $H^N$ is a closed $G$-invariant subspace and so is its orthogonal complement ([[thm-orthogonal-decomposition-by-a-closed-subspace]], [[def-orthogonality-and-orthogonal-complement]]). Write $\\xi=\\xi_1+\\xi_2$ with $\\xi_1\\in H^N$, $\\xi_2\\in H^N{}^\\perp$. For each $q$, both summands are preserved by $\\pi(q)$, so $\\lVert\\pi(q)\\xi-\\xi\\rVert^2=\\lVert\\pi(q)\\xi_1-\\xi_1\\rVert^2+\\lVert\\pi(q)\\xi_2-\\xi_2\\rVert^2\\ge\\lVert\\pi(q)\\xi_2-\\xi_2\\rVert^2$. The restriction of $\\pi$ to $H^N{}^\\perp$ has no nonzero $N$-invariant vector, so by the relative Kazhdan pair property it has no $(Q,\\varepsilon)$-invariant unit vector; hence $\\sup_{q\\in Q}\\lVert\\pi(q)\\xi_2-\\xi_2\\rVert\\ge\\varepsilon\\lVert\\xi_2\\rVert=\\varepsilon\\operatorname{dist}(\\xi,H^N)$. Combining with the displayed inequality gives the first bound. For the second, $\\pi(n)\\xi_1=\\xi_1$ for $n\\in N$, so $\\lVert\\pi(n)\\xi-\\xi\\rVert=\\lVert\\pi(n)\\xi_2-\\xi_2\\rVert\\le2\\lVert\\xi_2\\rVert$. For the final clause, suppose no $\\delta$ works; then for each $m$ there is a representation $\\pi_m$ without nonzero $N$-invariant vector and a unit vector $\\xi_m$ with $\\sup_{q\\in Q}\\lVert\\pi_m(q)\\xi_m-\\xi_m\\rVert<1/m$. The Hilbert direct sum $\\pi=\\widehat\\bigoplus_m\\pi_m$ ([[def-hilbert-direct-sum-of-unitary-representations]]) has no nonzero $N$-invariant vector, because the coordinate projections intertwine $G$ and a nonzero invariant vector would have a nonzero invariant coordinate. Since $Q$ generates $G$, every compact $C\\subseteq G$ is contained in some $Q^k$ (the compact sets $Q^k$ increase and cover $G$, so finitely many cover $C$); telescoping the displacement over the $k$ factors gives $\\sup_{C}\\lVert\\pi(g)\\xi_m-\\xi_m\\rVert<k/m$, so $\\pi$ has almost invariant vectors. Relative property (T) then yields a nonzero $N$-invariant vector, a contradiction. AC is used to select the counterexamples for all $m$ ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]).",
