        "statement": "Let $n\\ge2$ and for $1\\le i\\ne j\\le n$ and $t\\in\\mathbb R$ write $E_{ij}(t):=I_n+tE_{ij}$ for the elementary transvection ([[def-elementary-matrix]], [[def-elementary-row-operations-and-row-equivalence]], [[def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix]]). Then every $g\\in\\mathrm{SL}_n(\\mathbb R)$ ([[ex-general-and-special-linear-lie-groups]]) is a product of at most $M(n):=2n^2+6n$ elementary transvections $E_{ij}(t)$ (factors $E_{ij}(0)=I$ allowed). In particular $\\mathrm{SL}_n(\\mathbb R)$ is boundedly generated by its elementary root subgroups $H_{ij}=\\{E_{ij}(t):t\\in\\mathbb R\\}$.",
        "strategy": "Gaussian elimination with an explicit count. (1) For $k=1,\\dots,n-1$, operating only on rows and columns $k,\\dots,n$: if the $(k,k)$-entry of the current trailing block is zero, then, since that block is invertible, some entry of its first row or column is nonzero; one transvection of the form $E_{k,\\ell}(1)$ or $E_{\\ell,k}(1)$ makes the pivot nonzero. Then clear the remaining entries of row $k$ by right multiplications $E_{k,j}(-M_{kj}/M_{kk})$ and of column $k$ by left multiplications $E_{i,k}(-M_{ik}/M_{kk})$; these operations do not disturb the earlier rows and columns because the already cleared entries are zero. This costs at most $2(n-k)+1$ transvections at stage $k$, so at most $n^2-1$ altogether, and it produces $UgV=\\operatorname{diag}(d_1,\\dots,d_n)$ with $U,V$ products of at most $n^2-1$ transvections and $\\prod_id_i=1$ ([[cor-a-row-reduction-is-a-product-of-elementary-matrices]], [[cor-elementary-matrices-are-invertible]], [[thm-matrix-multiplication-laws]], [[def-determinant-of-a-square-matrix]]). (2) With $s_k=d_1\\cdots d_k$ one has the identity $\\operatorname{diag}(d_1,\\dots,d_n)=\\prod_{k=1}^{n-1}D_k$, where $D_k$ equals $s_k$ in position $(k,k)$, $s_k^{-1}$ in position $(k+1,k+1)$ and $1$ elsewhere; and for $a\\ne0$, using $t_+(a)=I+aI$ in the two positions $(k,k+1)$ and $t_-(b)=I+bI$ in $(k+1,k)$, the identity $t_+(a)\\,t_-(-a^{-1})\\,t_+(a)\\,\\bigl(t_-(1)\\,t_+(-1)\\,t_-(1)\\bigr)=\\operatorname{diag}(a,a^{-1})$ holds in the block, as one checks by multiplying the three factors; hence each $D_k$ is a product of $6$ transvections and the diagonal remainder is a product of $6(n-1)$ transvections. (3) Inverting the finite products of (1) multiplies the count by two, and inverses of transvections are transvections, so $g$ is a product of at most $2(n^2-1)+6(n-1)=2n^2+6n-8\\le M(n)$ transvections. This is the real-field Gaussian-elimination argument; it uses no bounded-generation theorem over the integers and no choice principle.",
